    {
      "path": ".netlify/task-history/attempt-1.md",
      "content": "# Task History - Attempt 1\n\n## Request - what the user asked for\nok! this is getting so close. our layout of the presentation/collab view seems off. please take a look at this screenshot and mend. if any key design decisions are needed, please let me know\n\n---\n\n## Response - what the agent replied with after its work\n\nThe presentation/collab view layout had two root causes making it appear off:\n\n**1. Broken flex-direction: row layout with the header**\n\nWhen the Conversations chat panel was open, the `.presentation-fullpage` container switched from `flex-direction: column` to `flex-direction: row`. This placed the sticky header element as a side-by-side flex item alongside the content area and chat panel, rather than spanning the full width at the top. The header competed for horizontal space in the row, pushing the content area to the right and leaving visible background animation on the left side.\n\nThis was fixed by replacing the `flex-direction: row` approach with a CSS Grid layout for the `.ucp-open` state. The grid uses `grid-template-columns: 1fr auto` and `grid-template-rows: auto 1fr`, which places the header in the first row spanning both columns (`grid-column: 1 / -1`), while the content area and chat panel sit side-by-side in the second row. The header now correctly occupies full width at the top, and the content area fills the remaining height and width next to the chat panel.\n\nA mobile override (`max-width: 768px`) reverts to `display: flex; flex-direction: column` since the chat panel uses `position: fixed` overlay on small screens and does not need to participate in the grid layout.\n\n**2. Narrow 800px max-width constraint on content sections**\n\nThe `.itinerary-section` elements (header/collaborator row, add-item toolbar) and `.itinerary-items-list` had a `max-width: 800px` with `margin: 0 auto`, constraining all content to a narrow centered column. While the board-view grid already overrode this for the card container, the surrounding section containers remained narrow, creating a visual disconnect. Two overrides were added in `css/deferred.css` scoped to `.presentation-fullpage` to set `max-width: 100%` on both `.itinerary-section` and `.itinerary-items-list`, allowing all content to fill the available width in the presentation view.\n\n"
    },
    {
      "path": ".netlify/task-history/attempt-2.md",
      "content": "# Task History - Attempt 2\n\n## Request - what the user asked for\nok, now with those changes we are closer. add debug statements to assist. attached is the console, and also a screenshot\n\n---\n\n## Response - what the agent replied with after its work\n\nAdded comprehensive layout debug statements to the presentation/collab view to diagnose why the content area appears narrow and the background animation bleeds through on the left side when the Conversations chat panel (UCP) is open.\n\nThe screenshot showed the content column squeezed into a narrow strip with only one card visible at a time, while the console logs from the latest deployment confirmed the vitality engine and card rendering are working correctly (5 cards found, badges applied). This points to a CSS layout issue rather than a rendering/data issue.\n\n**Debug statements added to `components/presentation.js`:**\n\n- **Post-activation layout dump (rAF callback):** After `modal.classList.add('active')`, a `requestAnimationFrame` callback logs the computed layout dimensions of every key element: the modal overlay (display mode, grid-template-columns, grid-template-rows, dimensions), the `.presentation-content` area (width, max-width, min-width, display, bounding rect), the UCP panel (display, width, position, bounding rect), the header (width, grid-column), the scroll container, the items list (display, grid-template-columns, card count, divider count), the first card's dimensions, and each `.itinerary-section`'s max-width/margin. This captures the full layout tree in one shot.\n\n- **Inline style conflict check:** Right after `modal.style.display = 'flex'` is set, a debug log explicitly warns that this inline style may override the CSS rule `.presentation-fullpage.active.ucp-open { display: grid }`, and logs the computed display value. This is likely the root cause of the layout issue - inline `display:flex` has higher specificity than any CSS class rule, preventing the grid layout from activating.\n\n- **Parent chain width tracing in renderAllItems:** After `innerHTML` is set on the items list, a loop walks up the parent chain (5 levels) logging each parent's offsetWidth, computed width, max-width, and overflow. This reveals where width constraints are propagating from.\n\n- **Delayed layout check (1 second):** A `setTimeout` fires 1 second after `showPresentationView` completes, logging the final settled layout state including all card positions (left, top, width, height). This catches any layout shifts from async operations.\n\n- **Hide/show lifecycle logging:** `hidePresentationView` now logs the modal's classes and display state before cleanup, so the hide-then-reshow cycle during `syncUiWithUrl` initialization can be traced.\n\n**Debug statements added to `components/unifiedChatPanel.js`:**\n\n- **showUnifiedChatPanel:** Logs the overlay's class list before and after adding `ucp-open`, whether the `active` class is present (it won't be yet since UCP opens before the modal activates), the computed display mode, and the UCP panel's computed width/display.\n\n- **hideUnifiedChatPanel:** Logs the overlay's class list before and after removing `ucp-open`.\n\n**Preliminary finding from code analysis:**\n\nThe most likely root cause is that `modal.style.display = 'flex'` (line 11976 in presentation.js) sets an inline style that overrides the CSS rule `.presentation-fullpage.active.ucp-open { display: grid }`. Inline styles always win over class-based selectors regardless of specificity. The debug logs will confirm this - if the computed display shows `flex` instead of `grid` when both `active` and `ucp-open` classes are present, that confirms the fix would be to either remove the inline display style or change it to use `!important` in the CSS (or clear the inline style after adding the class).\n\n"
    },
    {
      "path": "ARCHITECTURE.md",
      "content": "# Application Architecture Overview\nThis document outlines the technical architecture of the interactive event catalog.\nThe application is built on a modular architecture to ensure maintainability and scalability. Each JavaScript file has a distinct responsibility, separating concerns like data management, API communication, user interaction, and UI rendering.\n\n## Core Modules\n- **main.js:** The primary entry point for the public-facing application. Orchestrates initial setup, data fetching, event listeners, and the first UI render.\n- **teammate.js:** The primary entry point for the internal teammate profile page. Fetches and renders performance data for a specific teammate.\n- **events.js:** A centralized module that handles all user interactions, containing event listeners for clicks, form inputs, and other user-driven events.\n- **filtering.js:** Contains the complete logic for filtering and sorting the catalog based on user selections.\n- **api.js:** Manages all communication with external services like Airtable and Cloudinary.\n- **state.js:** Acts as a centralized, in-memory database for the application's current state.\n- **config.js:** A simple module for storing global constants and configuration values.\n- **session.js:** A minimal module, formerly used for managing session history in `localStorage`. This functionality has been deprecated in favor of a database-driven approach.\n\n## UI Modules\n- **ui.js:** The central \"hub\" for the user interface, importing and re-exporting functions from component modules. It also contains general-purpose UI functions.\n- **components/card.js:** Responsible for creating and rendering all card-based elements.\n- **components/modal.js:** Manages the functionality of the detail and checkout modals.\n- **components/presentation.js:** The presentation/plan view module. Renders items as a responsive CSS Grid of compact card tiles. Each card shows a hero photo with floating status badge (top-left), summary emoji (top-right), and lifecycle state badge (bottom-right for goal/locked/archived/completed). The card body displays the item name with an entry source-type badge (AI/solution/manual), merged-entry provenance as color-coded source pills, variation pills with lifecycle-aware styling, and a bottom meta bar. The meta bar contains a compact reaction bar showing the top 3 emojis as styled pill badges with counts, a task status badge (GTG/Check/Needs Attention/No Action) as a color-coded pill, and a comment count badge. Option group cards aggregate member lifecycle states via status chips, and also surface aggregate comment counts, combined reaction bars, and task status summaries across all group members. Clicking any card opens the detail modal directly. Drag-and-drop is powered by SortableJS with compact cards as direct draggable elements in grid mode. Cards can be reordered by dragging within the grid, with custom order persisted to session state. A radial menu appears on horizontal swipe or long-press, positioning itself around the card center with action buckets for lifecycle changes, reactions, comments, and deletion. Card-to-card merge detection uses dwell-time tracking — hovering a dragged card over another for 250ms activates merge mode with zone-aware visual feedback: dragging over the photo area triggers a hybrid merge (purple highlight), while the body area triggers an options merge (green highlight). The board features an enhanced empty state with category icons and a single-item nudge, staggered card entrance animations, ARIA accessibility (roles, labels, keyboard navigation), responsive touch targets, GPU-accelerated transforms with automatic cleanup, and support for prefers-reduced-motion and forced-colors media queries.\n- **components/sidebar.js:** Controls the right-hand \"Event Plan\" panel and the \"Favorites\" carousel.\n- **components/backgroundEngine.js:** The central engine that manages the animated WebGL canvas background and its reactive \"energy\" state.\n- **components/effects/fluid.js:** The WebGL shader plugin that renders the \"Fluid Energy\" vortex.\n\n## Serverless Functions (`netlify/functions/`)\n- **/api/process-email:** Receives email data from a webhook, uses the Gemini AI API to parse for sales information, and creates or updates records in the Airtable CRM.\n- **/api/auth-start & /api/auth-verify:** Handle the passwordless \"magic link\" authentication flow.\n- **/api/update-user-prefs:** Updates an authenticated user's record in Airtable with their notification preferences, such as phone number.\n- **/api/send-notification:** Triggered when a new chat message is posted. It fetches collaborators for the session and sends real-time SMS alerts via Twilio to users who have opted in.\n- **Other Functions:** Proxies for Calendar, Cloudinary, Payments, and Chat services.\n\n## Utility Modules\n- **utils/debug.js:** Provides a simple, toggleable logging system for development.\n- **utils/shader.js:** A minimal helper class to compile and run WebGL shader programs.\n"
    },
